---
import SubpageLayout from '@layouts/SubpageLayout.astro';

const _body = `
<section class="post-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="post-hero-meta">
      <a href="/blog.html" class="section-label" style="text-decoration:none">← Blog</a>
      <span class="post-date">September 29, 2026</span>
      <span class="badge">Architecture</span>
      <span class="badge">Capabilities</span>
      <span class="badge">Honesty</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="t-h1">Where business logic lives (hosts stay thin)</h1>
    <div class="post-byline">
      <span>By <strong>Enrico Piovesan</strong></span>
      <span>7 min read</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="container">
  <div class="post-layout">

    <article class="post-prose">

      <p>Cold readers keep landing on the same wrong picture: Traverse is a bag of tiny string utils, so real domain rules should stay in the host forever. That is not the product. This post is the narrative companion to the citeable Q&amp;A — <a href="/questions/where-does-business-logic-live-in-traverse.html">Where does business logic live in Traverse?</a> — for when you need the argument in order, not a one-screen answer.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        Direction stays one loop: <strong>discover → execute → trace</strong>. Non-UI domain rules belong in capabilities. Hosts and embedders do UI and I/O. The agent proposes; the runtime decides.
      </div>

      <h2 id="capability-vs-host">Capability vs host</h2>

      <p>A capability is a versioned contract plus a sandboxed WASM artifact. Pricing caps. Eligibility. Consent checks. Mesh codecs. Overhang analysis. Orientation scoring. Export gates. Anything that must behave the same way for every caller and leave a trace.</p>

      <p>The host — browser app, native shell, CLI, MCP client — renders UI, holds session state, and supplies the I/O the capability is allowed to use. It is not the forever home of the domain rule.</p>

      <p>If you are choosing where a rule should live, ask one question: would it be wrong for two hosts to disagree? If yes, put it in a capability.</p>

      <h2 id="utilities-not-ceiling">Utilities teach the pipe — they are not the ceiling</h2>

      <p>The registry includes small helpers: slugify, truncate, email validate. Those are legitimate. They teach the publish pipeline, the CI gates, and the review path. They are <strong>not</strong> the product identity and not a size limit.</p>

      <p>Preferred publishes are business-shaped: deterministic decisions, domain transforms, policy gates. “One small capability” means <strong>one concern per package</strong>, not “keep it tiny forever.” Packaging size is packaging. It is not a ceiling on how rich the rule can be.</p>

      <h2 id="apps-not-ready">“Apps are not ready” is sequencing, not expressiveness</h2>

      <p>When help-wanted tickets say apps are not ready, they mean something narrow: do not open a PR that rewrites a whole product UI or ports an entire application shell into this org’s capability repos. Capability packs are in scope. Domain logic in WASM is the point.</p>

      <p>It does <strong>not</strong> mean “keep the rules in JavaScript until some future milestone.” That reading turns a GTM / contribution-sequencing note into a technical no. It is the wrong conclusion.</p>

      <p>A living example of a domain pack (capabilities only): the print-support initiative — umbrella <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/issues/596" target="_blank" rel="noopener">registry#596</a>, Announcement <a href="https://github.com/orgs/traverse-framework/discussions/1540" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discussion #1540</a>. Codecs, scorers, fins/braces, export gates — as governed capabilities. Not a product shell PR.</p>

      <h2 id="deny-io">Deny-by-default I/O limits authority, not richness</h2>

      <p>Capabilities do not get ambient filesystem or network access. Today’s common execution boundary is stdin/stdout JSON through the embedder. That limits <em>authority</em>. It does not limit how rich the rule can be.</p>

      <p>Putting pricing or eligibility back into React “because WASM cannot touch disk” is the wrong conclusion. Call the capability. Keep the host thin.</p>

      <p>Same correction for the React guide’s “app never touches Rust/WASM directly.” That means go through the published embedder client (<code>traverse-embedder-web</code>). It does <strong>not</strong> mean reimplement the business rule in React. See the <a href="/docs/guides/react-integration.html">React integration guide</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="authoring">How to put a rule in a capability</h2>

      <p>Usual path: plain English via the Claude skill <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/claude-skills/tree/main/skills/traverse-capability-author" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>traverse-capability-author</code></a> — interview → registry check → contract → executable WASM → human-reviewed PR. Under the hood that still compiles Rust to WASM. You do not need to write Rust for the usual path.</p>

      <p>More detail: <a href="/blog/you-dont-need-rust-to-publish-a-capability.html">You don't need Rust to publish a capability</a> and <a href="/questions/how-do-i-author-a-capability-in-plain-english.html">How do I author in plain English?</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="what-this-is-not">What this is not</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Not “Traverse is only tiny utils.”</li>
        <li>Not “leave domain logic in the host forever.”</li>
        <li>Not a promise that every OS host or every I/O shape is shipped — check <a href="/platforms.html">Platforms</a> and <a href="/what-is-real-today.html">what is real today</a>.</li>
        <li>Not an invitation to invent a Python SDK or a second runtime. One shared <code>runtime.wasm</code>; Python shells out to the CLI today.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        Prefer the Q&amp;A when you need a URL to quote. Prefer this post when you need the story. For the project direction in one scene, read the <a href="/blog/agent-freestyle-blocked.html">deny-demo blog</a> — agent freestyle → blocked, with a trace.
      </div>

    </article>

    <aside class="post-sidebar">
      <div class="sidebar-card">
        <div class="sidebar-title">In this post</div>
        <ul class="toc-list">
          <li><a href="#capability-vs-host">Capability vs host</a></li>
          <li><a href="#utilities-not-ceiling">Utilities ≠ ceiling</a></li>
          <li><a href="#apps-not-ready">Apps not ready</a></li>
          <li><a href="#deny-io">Deny-by-default I/O</a></li>
          <li><a href="#authoring">How to author</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-this-is-not">What this is not</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="sidebar-card">
        <div class="sidebar-title">Related</div>
        <ul class="related-list">
          <li><a href="/questions/where-does-business-logic-live-in-traverse.html">Where does business logic live? →</a></li>
          <li><a href="/what-is-real-today.html">What is real today →</a></li>
          <li><a href="/blog/you-dont-need-rust-to-publish-a-capability.html">Skill-first authoring →</a></li>
          <li><a href="/blog/agent-freestyle-blocked.html">Deny demo blog →</a></li>
          <li><a href="/blog/what-is-real-today-start-here.html">Start here narrative →</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="sidebar-card sidebar-cta">
        <div class="sidebar-title">Cite the Q&amp;A</div>
        <p>Capabilities hold non-UI rules. Hosts stay thin. Utilities teach the pipe — they are not the ceiling.</p>
        <a href="/questions/where-does-business-logic-live-in-traverse.html" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm" style="width:100%;text-align:center;display:block">Where logic lives →</a>
      </div>
    </aside>

  </div>
</div>

<section class="cta-band">
  <div class="container" style="text-align:center">
    <h2 class="t-h2" style="margin-bottom:0.75rem">Hosts stay thin. Rules stay governed.</h2>
    <p class="t-body t-muted" style="margin-bottom:2rem;max-width:480px;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto">Discover → execute → trace. Skill-first authoring. One shared runtime. Domain logic in capabilities.</p>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <a href="/questions/where-does-business-logic-live-in-traverse.html" class="btn btn-primary">Where logic lives →</a>
      <a href="/blog/agent-freestyle-blocked.html" class="btn btn-ghost">Deny demo blog</a>
      <a href="/what-is-real-today.html" class="btn btn-ghost">What is real today</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
`;
---
<SubpageLayout
  title={"Where business logic lives (hosts stay thin) — Traverse Blog"}
  description={"Narrative companion to the where-logic Q&A: non-UI domain rules in capabilities, hosts = UI + I/O, utilities ≠ ceiling, apps-not-ready ≠ leave logic in the host."}
  canonical={"https://traverse-framework.com/blog/where-business-logic-lives.html"}
  crumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Blog", href: "/blog.html" }]}
>
  <style is:global slot="head">.post-hero-meta { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .post-date { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--fg-muted); }
    .post-hero h1 { max-width: 760px; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
    .post-byline { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 1.5rem; font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--fg-muted); flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .post-byline strong { color: var(--fg); font-weight: 500; }
    .post-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 280px; gap: 4rem; padding: 3.5rem 0 5rem; align-items: start; }
    @media (max-width: 900px) { .post-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2.5rem; } .post-sidebar { order: -1; } }
    .post-prose { max-width: 680px; font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.75; color: var(--fg); }
    .post-prose p { margin: 0 0 1.4rem; }
    .post-prose h2 { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 600; margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem; color: var(--fg); }
    .post-prose ol, .post-prose ul { margin: 0 0 1.4rem 1.25rem; }
    .post-prose li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
    .post-prose a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--accent-dim); }
    .post-prose a:hover { text-decoration-color: var(--accent); }
    .post-prose code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85em; background: var(--bg-elevated); padding: 0.15em 0.4em; border-radius: 4px; color: var(--accent); }
    .callout { background: var(--bg-elevated); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.75rem 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--fg-muted); }
    .post-sidebar { position: sticky; top: calc(var(--nav-h) + 2rem); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem; }
    .sidebar-card { background: var(--bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.25rem; }
    .sidebar-title { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 600; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--fg-muted); margin-bottom: 0.9rem; }
    .toc-list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.4rem; }
    .toc-list a { font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.83rem; color: var(--fg-muted); text-decoration: none; display: block; padding: 0.2rem 0.6rem; border-left: 2px solid transparent; transition: color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s; }
    .toc-list a:hover { color: var(--fg); border-left-color: var(--accent); }
    .related-list { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.6rem; }
    .related-list a { font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.83rem; color: var(--fg-muted); text-decoration: none; line-height: 1.4; }
    .related-list a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
    .sidebar-cta { background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bg-elevated), var(--bg-card)); border-color: var(--accent-dim); }
    .sidebar-cta p { font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.83rem; color: var(--fg-muted); margin: 0 0 1rem; line-height: 1.55; }</style>
  <Fragment set:html={_body} />
</SubpageLayout>
